"explanation.auth" = "This is how the request proves who you are. A username and password get scrambled into one Authorization header; a token is sent in that same header after the word \"Bearer\". Either way it is just a header, which is why you can still see it in the Headers section.";
"explanation.auth.title" = "Signing in";
"explanation.body" = "The body is the content you are sending along with the request — the parcel rather than the label. GET requests usually have none; POST and PUT usually do.";
"explanation.body.title" = "Body";
"explanation.contentType" = "Content-Type is the header that tells the server what kind of thing is in the body, so it knows how to read it — JSON, form fields, plain text, a file, and so on.";
"explanation.contentType.title" = "Content-Type";
"explanation.cookies" = "Cookies are small notes a website asked your browser to keep and hand back on every visit. They are how a site remembers that you are signed in. They travel in a single header, one note after another.";
"explanation.cookies.title" = "Cookies";
"explanation.formBody" = "A form body is a list of name-and-value pairs, written the same way as a query string: name=value, joined by \"&\". It is what an ordinary web page form sends when you press a button.";
"explanation.formBody.title" = "Form fields";
"explanation.headers" = "Headers are extra lines of information attached to the request, like the label on a parcel. They say things like what kind of answer you can accept and what software is asking. Switch one off to see what the site does without it.";
"explanation.headers.title" = "Headers";
"explanation.json" = "JSON is a common way of writing structured data as text, using braces for objects and square brackets for lists. Most web APIs expect it. If it is laid out neatly here, it still arrives as one long line.";
"explanation.json.title" = "JSON";
"explanation.method" = "The method is the verb of the request: it says what you want done. GET means \"give me this\", POST usually means \"here is something new\", PUT and PATCH mean \"change this\", and DELETE means what it says.";
"explanation.method.title" = "Method";
"explanation.otherOptions" = "These are curl's own settings rather than parts of the request: how to handle redirects, whether to unzip the answer, how long to wait. Anything curly does not recognise is kept here untouched so nothing is lost.";
"explanation.otherOptions.title" = "Other options";
"explanation.output" = "This is the finished command. Copy it, paste it into your terminal, and press return to run it. Send runs it here instead, which is handy, but the command is the thing worth keeping. Edit anything on the left and watch the matching part here change.";
"explanation.output.title" = "The curl command";
"explanation.query" = "The query string is the part of the address after the \"?\". It is a list of name-and-value pairs, joined by \"&\", used for things like search terms and page numbers. Spaces and other awkward characters get written as %20 and friends so they survive the trip.";
"explanation.query.title" = "Query string";
"explanation.request" = "A request is one message your computer sends to a website, asking it to do something. Everything below is a part of that message. curly's job is to write that message down so you can read it; Send will run it for you, but the command is still the thing to keep.";
"explanation.request.title" = "The request";
"explanation.url" = "The web address says which machine to talk to and which thing on it you want. It is made of a scheme (https), a host (api.example.com), sometimes a port number, and a path (/v1/items).";
"explanation.url.title" = "Web address";
"explanation.verbosity" = "By default curl prints what came back and nothing else. You can ask it for more: the response headers as well as the body, or the whole conversation including what your machine sent. Turning this up is the quickest way to see what a request actually looks like.";
"explanation.verbosity.title" = "How much to show";
